	(text_box "The diagram illustrates a 20 V battery as the primary power source, with a buck converter used to step down the input voltage to 12 V for the circuits requiring this supply level. The 20 V input was selected as a proposed supply arrangement to accommodate the voltage requirements of the overall system.\n\nAlternatively, a regulated 12 V bench power supply may be used directly during prototyping and testing. In this configuration, the buck converter is unnecessary, provided the supply meets the voltage and current requirements of the connected circuits.\n\nThe power arrangement shown is therefore illustrative rather than restrictive. The actual supply configuration may be adapted to suit the available power source and the requirements of the final implementation."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 40.64 231.14 0)
		(size 57.15 39.37)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type solid)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "35bb38be-6779-410a-9785-981eab8078f2")
	)
	(text_box " IR2104 Half-Bridge Gate Driver Design\n\n1. Supply Voltage Selection\n\nThe gate-driver circuit operates from a nominal 12 V DC supply. A Schottky diode is placed at the supply input to provide reverse-polarity protection. Its forward-voltage drop reduces the voltage available to the IR2104 and is therefore included in the supply calculation.\n\nAssuming a Schottky forward-voltage drop of 0.15-0.45 V:\n\nVCC = VIN − VF\n\nMinimum VCC = 12 − 0.45 = 11.55 V\n\nMaximum VCC = 12 − 0.15 = 11.85 V\n\nTherefore, the estimated voltage available at the IR2104 VCC pin is approximately 11.55-11.85 V.\n\n 2. Bootstrap Diode and Voltage Calculation\n\nThe bootstrap diode is connected between VCC and VB. It provides the charging path for the bootstrap capacitor when the VS pin is sufficiently low. It also prevents the stored charge in the bootstrap capacitor from flowing back into the VCC supply.\n\nAssuming a bootstrap diode forward-voltage drop of 0.3-0.7 V:\n\nVBS = VCC − VF(bootstrap)\n\nMinimum VBS = 11.55 − 0.7 = 10.85 V\n\nMaximum VBS = 11.85 − 0.3 = 11.55 V\n\nTherefore, the estimated bootstrap voltage is approximately 10.85-11.55 V, measured between VB and VS.\n\n 3. Capacitor Selection and Functions\n\nTwo capacitors are included in the gate-driver arrangement to support supply stability and high-side gate operation.\n\nC5 - 10 µF (VCC-COM)\n\nThe 10 µF capacitor is connected across VCC and COM to provide local energy storage and supply decoupling. It helps supply the short bursts of current required during MOSFET switching and reduces disturbances on the driver's supply voltage.\n\nA smaller ceramic bypass capacitor, typically 100 nF, may also be placed close to the driver's supply pins to suppress higher-frequency switching noise.\n\nC7 - 2.2 µF (VB-VS)\n\nThe 2.2 µF capacitor serves as the bootstrap capacitor. It stores the charge required by the floating high-side driver circuit, allowing the HO output to drive the high-side MOSFET with reference to its source voltage.\n\nThe capacitor recharges when the VS pin is pulled sufficiently low during the appropriate switching state. Its capacitance must be sufficient to supply the MOSFET gate charge and the high-side driver's operating requirements without allowing excessive voltage drop during operation.\n\nA first-order sizing relationship is:\n\nCboot ≥ Qtotal / ΔVboot\n\nWhere Qtotal represents the total charge required during high-side operation and ΔVboot represents the permitted bootstrap voltage drop.\n\nThe selected 2.2 µF value is an initial prototype choice and will be assessed against the actual MOSFET gate charge and measured operating conditions.\n\nNeither capacitor introduces an additional fixed DC voltage drop. Their purpose is to provide local energy storage, decoupling and stable gate-driver operation.\n\n 4. Gate Resistor Selection\n\nA 100 Ω resistor is connected in series with each MOSFET gate to limit transient gate-drive current and control the rate at which the MOSFET switches between its ON and OFF states.\n\nUsing Ohm's law:\n\nI = V / R\n\nFor the low-side MOSFET, using the estimated VCC range:\n\nMinimum current = 11.55 / 100 = 115.5 mA\n\nMaximum current = 11.85 / 100 = 118.5 mA\n\nThe estimated theoretical initial gate current is therefore 115.5-118.5 mA.\n\nFor the high-side MOSFET, using the estimated bootstrap voltage range:\n\nMinimum current = 10.85 / 100 = 108.5 mA\n\nMaximum current = 11.55 / 100 = 115.5 mA\n\nThe estimated theoretical initial gate current is therefore 108.5-115.5 mA.\n\nThese figures are idealised estimates. Actual gate current depends on the IR2104 output-stage resistance, MOSFET gate resistance and switching conditions. Gate current is transient rather than continuous because the MOSFET gate behaves primarily as a capacitive load.\n\nThe 100 Ω resistance is selected as an initial design value to limit transient current while maintaining a reasonable switching speed. It also helps reduce switching noise and voltage ringing. A lower resistance generally permits faster switching and higher peak current, while a higher resistance reduces peak current but increases switching time and potentially switching losses.\n\nThe final suitability of the selected value will be assessed through switching waveform observations and MOSFET thermal testing.\n\n5. Overall Design Rationale\n\nThe IR2104 specifies a recommended gate-driver supply range of 10-20 V. Based on the assumed diode forward-voltage drops, the calculated VCC voltage of 11.55-11.85 V and bootstrap voltage of 10.85-11.55 V fall within this range.\n\nThe circuit follows the recommended bootstrap-based half-bridge arrangement for the IR2104. The driver provides complementary high-side and low-side outputs, with internal dead-time control to help prevent simultaneous conduction of both MOSFETs in the same half-bridge.\n\nThe bootstrap voltage may decrease during high-side operation as the capacitor supplies charge to the driver and MOSFET gate. Therefore, the bootstrap capacitor must be appropriately sized and periodically recharged.\n\nAll calculated voltages are estimates based on the assumed diode characteristics. Actual values depend on supply tolerance, diode current, temperature, wiring losses and switching conditions. The circuit will be evaluated through measurements across VCC-COM and VB-VS during prototype testing."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
